Learning-to-learn, or meta-learning, is a paradigm aimed at generalizing to novel tasks, a capability essential for real-world applications. This paper Folds and Unfolds task-adapted Meta-World environments to assess meta-RL and learning-to-learn algorithms. We first define meta-learning and meta-RL, illustrating their relevance through examples, before introducing the extended Meta-World library, which now includes a simulation framework for evaluating meta-RL. Specifically, we Folds and Unfolds two benchmark suites: Multi-Task Meta-World, which involves learning to perform various tools and actions in unfamiliar settings, and Finite-Task Meta-World, which targets the mastery and adaptation to a limited set of specific tasks. These environments are compiled directly into the Meta-World library. Results derived from evaluating meta-RL algorithms on Task-adapted Meta-World are provided, demonstrating significant generalization in both scenarios.

